# D51 / `todo/t0718` — `unify`'s smart-pointer coercion is keyed on the TYPED
# builtin IDENTITY (`DefInfo.builtin_kind`), never on the definition's NAME.
#
# ⛔ THE TYPED AXIS IS THE THREE-NAME COERCION SET, AND EVERY REACHABILITY PATH
# TO IT OWES ALL THREE NAMES. Pre-fix these were three separate string literals
# (`name == "Mutex" || name == "Shared" || name == "RWLock"`), so a set that
# samples one of them is an anecdote about a list of three. Measured: all three
# change on BOTH routes and on BOTH questions — the product is real, not a
# convention.
#
# MESSAGE cell, not a verdict cell: rc 1 on BOTH sides, and only the BLAME
# moves. `Shared(5)` cannot infer `T` — a pre-existing, name-independent gap — but
# pre-fix the coercion arm answered `true` on the USER's `Shared` and unwrapped the
# annotation `Shared[int]` to `int` before reporting, naming a type the author
# never wrote:
#
#   pre-fix   error[E_TypeMismatch]: expected `int`,         found `Shared`
#   post-fix  error[E_TypeMismatch]: expected `Shared[int]`, found `Shared`
#
# Post-fix it matches the name-neutral control (`struct Wrap[T]` + `Wrap(5)`)
# exactly, which is the proof the message is no longer a function of the
# spelling. The forbidden string is the pre-fix spelling — ⚠ an rc sweep is
# STRUCTURALLY BLIND to this cell, which is how the whole message half of the
# axis went unpinned.
#
# Asserted by `coercion_identity_user_shared_ctor_blames_the_annotation`.

struct Shared[T]:
    T value

void main():
    Shared[int] m = Shared(5)
    print(1)
